@page "/docs/theming"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>Theming · Lux.UI</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Theming</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">Four levels, from the broadest to the most local: the palettes of the design system, the CSS variables, the global theme, and per-instance overrides.</p>

    <h2>Palettes</h2>
    <p>Map the semantic colors (<code>primary</code>, <code>neutral</code>…) to Tailwind palettes or your own, add colors of your own, and set a default color and size, all in <code>AddLuxUi</code>. Lux.UI computes the variables so the contrast rules still hold. See <a href="/docs/design-system">Design system</a>.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Palettes" />

    <h2>Design tokens</h2>
    <p>Colors, radius and surfaces are CSS variables. Override them in both <code>:root</code> and <code>.dark</code>, with or without Tailwind. <a href="/docs/css-variables">The whole list</a>, with the Tailwind utilities that follow it, is a page of its own.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="app.css" Code="@Tokens" />

    <h2>Custom colors</h2>
    <p>The easy way is <code>o.Colors.Add("tertiary", palette)</code> with <code>LxThemeStyle</code>. By hand, define the three variables for a name and pass it as a <code>Color</code>; this works with and without Tailwind.</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@CustomColor" />

    <h2>Global theme</h2>
    <p>Every component has slots, variants and defaults declared in C#. Classes you add win over the defaults.</p>
    <p>Configure themes in <code>AddLuxUi</code>, which may be called more than once (a library, then the app). A theme becomes read-only the first time a component renders with it, because resolved classes are cached: changing it afterwards throws an <code>InvalidOperationException</code>. The <code>Slots</code>, <code>Variants</code>, <code>CompoundVariants</code> and <code>DefaultVariants</code> properties are read-only views; use <code>Slot</code>, <code>Variant</code>, <code>Compound</code> and <code>Default</code> to configure. <code>LxTheme</code> scopes are unaffected, they only add classes per subtree. The palettes and the defaults of <code>o.Colors</code> and <code>o.Defaults</code> freeze the same way.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Global" />

    <h2>Per instance</h2>
    <p><code>Class</code> styles the root element; <code>Ui</code> targets any slot. Both take Tailwind utilities, so they need Tailwind compiled by your app.</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Instance" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string Palettes = """
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o =>
        {
            o.Colors.Primary = LxPalette.Blue;
            o.Colors.Neutral = LxPalette.Zinc;
            o.Colors.Add("tertiary", LxPalette.Indigo);
        });
        """;

    private const string Tokens = """
        :root {
          --lux-radius: 0.5rem;
          --lux-primary: var(--color-teal-700);
          --lux-primary-text: var(--color-teal-800);
        }
        .dark {
          --lux-primary: var(--color-teal-400);
          --lux-primary-text: var(--color-teal-300);
        }
        """;

    private const string CustomColor = """
        [data-color='brand'] { --lx-color: #0f766e; --lx-color-text: #115e59; --lx-color-contrast: #fff; }

        <LxButton Label="Brand button" Color="@("brand")" />
        """;

    private const string Global = """
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o =>
        {
            o.Button.Slot("base", "rounded-full");
            o.Card.Slot("root", "shadow-sm");
        });
        """;

    private const string Instance = """
        <LxButton Label="Save" Class="px-6" Ui="@(new ButtonUi { Label = "uppercase tracking-wide" })" />
        """;
}
